{"id": "6b6f0c2b-218d-48e4-ab62-c6118a3c302c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:48:10.920987", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f4cbd3db-3098-4216-8d70-65d6e5f9105f", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-445144ae-7898-4e54-8a10-f95444120530", "tool_call": {"id": "call-445144ae-7898-4e54-8a10-f95444120530", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Submit design-only plan stage (4 items)\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n## 1. Repository & context findings\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains a single file, `task.json` (570 B). No code, no model weights, no datasets, no `track.md` file body are present in the repository.\\n- `task.json.documents.track.md` is just the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" — i.e., context is the question itself, not bundled artefacts.\\n- Research topics in the task envelope reference `neurips2024/97431/5:setup_8` and `…:setup_9` (an external NeurIPS-style track). Those are *not* available inside `/workspace`, and the sandbox has no network, so their exact SSP wording cannot be retrieved here.\\n- Runtime probe (executed, not fabricated):\\n  - Python 3.12.3 interpreter present; no `torch`, `transformers`, `sklearn`, `numpy`. `pip3 list` shows only base OS packages.\\n  - 24 CPU cores, ~125 GiB RAM, ~770 GiB free on `/workspace`, 63 GiB tmpfs on `/tmp`.\\n  - Network disabled: DNS for `huggingface.co` and `pypi.org` fails; `bubblewrap` denies raw sockets.\\n  - Conclusion: the sandbox **cannot host the experiment itself**. The deliverable is a design whose *execution* is feasible in a normal downstream environment (network + GPU + deps), and an explicit threat-to-validity flag for the sandbox.\\n- No pre-emption of researcher judgement: setup_8 vs setup_9 content and the choice of judge are left as researcher decisions in §4.\\n\\n## 2. Proposed approach (high-level skeleton, NOT a full protocol)\\n\\n**Research question.** Does a Safety System Prompt (SSP) significantly reduce LLM Attack Success Rate (ASR) against jailbreaks, and is the effect moderated by attack family (token-level vs prompt-level)?\\n\\n**Hypotheses.**\\n- H1 (main effect): SSP-on yields lower ASR than SSP-off overall.\\n- H2 (within-family): SSP reduces ASR in both the token-level family and the prompt-level family.\\n- H3 (interaction): the SSP-induced ASR reduction is *larger* in the prompt-level family than in the token-level family (directed expectation, since template/prompt-level attacks primarily exploit the system-message contract, while gradient/lexical perturbations partially bypass it).\\n\\n**Independent variables.**\\n- IV1 — SSP: {off, on(setup_8), on(setup_9)}. Treat setup_8 and setup_9 as two matched-shape SSP variants so the design can also answer \\\"does the *specific* SSP content matter, beyond having any SSP?\\\".\\n- IV2 — Attack family: {token-level, prompt-level}.\\n- Within-cell: specific attack method (see below), seed (for stochastic attacks), prompt.\\n\\n**Dependent variable.**\\n- ASR per (SSP × attack family × attack method × prompt): binary jailbreak-success on each (prompt, attack) sample.\\n- Jailbreak-success judgment: dual-channel —\\n  (a) deterministic refusal/compliance lexicon pass (presence of refusal keywords vs. content match), used as a fast prefilter; and\\n  (b) an authoritative judge (open-source safety classifier such as Llama-Guard / HarmBench-classifier, or a stronger LLM judge at T=0). Final per-sample success = judge positive. When the two channels disagree, flag for review and report a sensitivity analysis (ASR under each channel separately).\\n- A secondary, *non-primary* measure: benign-helpfulness / over-refusal rate on a clean benign set (~50 prompts), used as a sanity check, not as the main outcome.\\n\\n**Candidate base models and rationale.**\\n- Preferred default: one open-weights instruction-tuned small model that *actually* consumes system messages, e.g. Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct or Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (CPU-runnable smoke test) and one mid-size model such as Mistral-7B-Instruct or Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (main result, GPU). Open weights are required so the experimenter can (i) reproduce exactly, (ii) grep the chat template to control order/placement, (iii) avoid vendor-side system-prompt rewriting that would confound the SSP effect.\\n- Rationale: instruction-tuned models are the realistic deployment target for SSP and are shaped to obey a system role. Closed-API endpoints are excluded as primary cells because providers may silently inject their own pre-prompt, breaking the IV.\\n\\n**Candidate attack methods (paired within each family).**\\n- Token-level (perturb the input tokens adversarially against model logits):\\n  1. GCG (greedy coordinate-gradient) suffix optimization — canonical, costly.\\n  2. BEA / GBDA / AutoDAN-style discrete search — lighter, paired as a feasibility cross-check.\\n  Adversary budget: identical step count / wall-clock for SSP-on vs SSP-off (e.g., 500 GCG steps), same prompt, same random seed.\\n- Prompt-level (in-context template attacks, no gradient access):\\n  1. Persona-override family (DAN-style / \\\"Developer Mode\\\" / AIM) — confusable-twin templates from prior literature.\\n  2. Instruction-injection family (\\\"Ignore previous instructions\\\", payload splitting, role spoof, multi-turn escalation).\\n  3. Encoding/cipher-wrap (Base64 / ArtPrompt-style) — bounded to English-domain variants.\\n- Each attack is instantiated as a *paired* comparison: same harmful goal, same attack instance up to the SSP slot being toggled.\\n\\n**Candidate SSP content shape (setup_8 vs setup_9).**\\n- Both treated as fixed-text system-role messages supplied by the researcher; treated as two alternative SSP variants with comparable length and tone. Length-controlled filler SSP (neutral but matching token count) is added as an auxiliary cell so a positive SSP effect cannot be reduced to \\\"more tokens in front of the user prompt\\\".\\n- Without network, the exact setup_8/setup_9 wording is not retrievable here; this is itemised under §4.\\n\\n**Datasets / prompts.**\\n- Harmful goals: a static published set — AdvBench `harmful_behaviors` (~520), HarmBench standard (~400), or JailbreakBench (~100). Pick one canonical source as the primary, used identically across all cells; optionally replicate on a second source for robustness.\\n- Benign sanity set: ~50 prompts from Alpaca-Eval / MT-Bench style sources.\\n- Pairing across SSP±: identical harmful-goal IDs across cells → paired statistical tests.\\n\\n**Controls & confounds.**\\n- Model version: pin weights commit hash; reload only once per session.\\n- Decoding: greedy, T=0, fixed `max_new_tokens` (e.g., 256), identical across cells.\\n- Chat template: pinned per model, identical across cells.\\n- System-prompt placement & ordering: always first system-role slot; never multiplexed with the user message; no auxiliary hidden system prompts.\\n- Attack budget: discrete step / wall-clock cap per attack, identical across SSP± cells; report actual budget and convergence check.\\n- Prompt length: token-count and visible-content length tracked; in SSP-off cells a length-matched neutral prefix is *not* inserted for the token-level attack (it would itself be attackable) but is used in a sensitivity cell.\\n- Randomisation: randomise prompt order; counterbalance SSP-on/off order to avoid drift over a long run.\\n- Judge determinism: judge at T=0; if an LLM judge is used, its own decoding is held constant.\\n\\n**Replication / sample size.**\\n- Per cell: N = 100 harmful prompts (from the chosen canonical set); for stochastic attacks K ≥ 3 random seeds averaged at the prompt level or treated as replicates.\\n- Paired design (same prompt in SSP-on and SSP-off within attack family) → McNemar-style paired tests; this is the smallest reasonable cell for stable ASR CIs.\\n- With N = 100 and a paired McNemar test, an ASR shift from ~0.60 (off) to ~0.30 (on) is detectable at power > 0.9 (α = 0.05). Smaller shifts require N ≈ 300 — report this as a power note.\\n\\n**Analysis plan.**\\n- Per-cell ASR with Wilson 95% CIs; benign over-refusal rate with the same treatment.\\n- Primary test within attack family: McNemar's test on paired binary jailbreak-success, with Bonferroni or Holm correction across (family × attack method) cells.\\n- Effect sizes: odds ratio from the paired 2×2, Cohen's h for proportion difference, ARR = ASR_off − ASR_on.\\n- Interaction (H3): mixed-effects logistic regression on the binary observation with fixed effects `SSP`, `family`, `SSP×family`, and a prompt-level random intercept; robust/cluster SEs by prompt. Signed sign on the interaction coefficient is the formal H3 test.\\n- Sensitivity: ASR computed under (i) judge-only, (ii) lexicon-only, (iii) union, (iv) intersection — to expose judge-bias sensitivity.\\n\\n**Threats to validity.**\\n- Internal: judge bias and lexicon false positives/negatives; GCG seed sensitivity; greedy-decoding variance ≠ deployment variance.\\n- Construct: ASR is meaningless without a specified judge; SSP can shift behaviour toward over-refusal instead of true robustness — explored via the benign-helpfulness sanity check.\\n- External: English-only harmful prompts; small open-source models ≠ the proprietary systems users care about; attack libraries drift over time.\\n- Runtime-environment specific: this sandbox (no network, no GPU, no ML libs) cannot actually execute the experiment. The design targets a downstream normal environment; any future claim of \\\"results\\\" must come from that environment, not this one.\\n\\n**Feasibility (in a downstream normal env).**\\n- Prompt-level cells (3 templates × 100 prompts × 2 SSP × 1 model ≈ 600 generations): a few GPU-hours.\\n- Token-level cells (GCG 500 steps × 100 prompts × 2 SSP × 1 model): on the order of 1–3 days on a single A100 for an 8B model; BEA/AutoDAN is ~10× cheaper as a fast first-pass check.\\n- Pure-CPU smoke test on a 1–3B model is possible for the prompt-level family only; GCG on CPU at N = 100 is not realistic.\\n\\n## 3. Consequential assumptions (made to scope this design)\\n\\n1. setup_8 and setup_9 are externally defined SSP content variants from the referenced NeurIPS track; the design treats them as alternates whose exact wording is researcher-supplied (not retrievable in this offline sandbox). Until the text is provided, the SSP-on cells are placeholders.\\n2. ASR is operationally defined as the *judge's* per-prompt binary jailbreak-success verdict; a strong external/open judge (Llama-Guard, HarmBench-classifier, or GPT-class judge at T=0) is assumed available in the downstream env.\\n3. Attack family is reduced to two levels — token-level (GCG + a lighter discrete search twin) and prompt-level (persona-override + instruction-injection + cipher-wrap templates) — and is assumed to be representative enough to support in-family paired tests.\\n4. Decoding is greedy (T=0) in the primary analysis, trading deployment realism for variance control; a sampling-decoding sensitivity cell is permitted but not required for the primary result.\\n5. SSP is placed as the *first* system-role message and is held constant in position/format across all SSP-on cells; any observed effect is attributed to SSP *content*, not to where/how it is delivered.\\n6. Refusal-vs-safety-true-robustness is partially disambiguated by measuring over-refusal on a benign set; the design assumes a single paired benign set is sufficient as a sanity check, not a co-primary outcome.\\n\\n## 4. Key uncertainties requiring researcher / clinician judgement before I proceed\\n\\n1. **Exact contents of setup_8 and setup_9.** Are they (a) two competing SSP variants, (b) one SSP and one baseline, or (c) two SSPs at different lengths/tones? Without the wording, SSP-on cannot be specified and length-controlled baselines are ambiguous.\\n2. **Base-model scope.** Is one small open-source model acceptable as the primary cell, or do you require multiple model families for generalization? This trades ~10× compute for ecological breadth.\\n3. **Canonical harmful-prompt corpus.** AdvBench, HarmBench-standard, or JailbreakBench — which is the reference set for this question? Will any cells be replicated on a second corpus?\\n4. **Judge of record.** Lexicon-only, open-source classifier (Llama-Guard / HarmBench classifier), or external LLM judge (GPT-class)? This is the single largest construct-validity lever in the entire ASR definition.\\n5. **Compute budget and attack-step cap.** GCG at 500 vs 100 steps changes feasibility by ~5×; legitimate comparison with prior SSP-defence papers requires matching their published step counts.\\n6. **Adversary surface for token-level attacks.** Does the attacker perturb only the user message, or is the system prompt also in the editable surface? A stronger adversary model materially raises expected ASR and weakens H1.\\n7. **Benign-helpfulness role.** Is over-refusal a co-equal primary outcome or strictly a sanity check? If co-equal, it should be pre-registered with its own N and its own significance rule.\\n8. **Scope of \\\"prompt-level\\\" family.** Should multi-turn / escalation attacks be included alongside single-turn template attacks? Multi-turn roughly doubles prompt-level design complexity and changes the unit of analysis.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"Findings (executed, observed): /workspace holds only task.json (570 B); track.md is referenced as the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" and is not present as a file. Python 3.12.3 is present; `import torch/transformers/sklearn/numpy` all fail with ModuleNotFoundError; pip3 list contains only base OS packages. Resources: nproc=24, free -h shows ~125 GiB RAM, df shows ~770 GiB free on /workspace. Network: `curl https://huggingface.co` and `curl https://pypi.org` both return \\\"Could not resolve host\\\"; `ping` denied (Operation not permitted) consistent with bubblewrap. No code or model artefacts are present to inspect; the referenced neurips2024/97431/5:setup_8 and setup_9 are not in /workspace and unreachable due to network being disabled, so their exact SSP contents cannot be retrieved here and are flagged as a researcher-supplied uncertainty. No experiment was executed; no ASR or other result has been claimed.\",\"assumptions\":\"Same as §3 of the answer: (1) setup_8/setup_9 are externally defined SSP variants whose exact wording is not retrievable in this offline sandbox; (2) ASR is defined as a per-prompt binary verdict from a strong external judge assumed available downstream; (3) attack family is dichotomised into token-level (GCG + lighter twin) and prompt-level (persona-override + injection + cipher-wrap); (4) primary decoding is greedy T=0; (5) SSP is always first system-role message with constant placement; (6) benign over-refusal is a sanity check, not a co-primary outcome.\",\"uncertainties\":\"(1) Exact wording/comparison intent of setup_8 vs setup_9 — researcher must supply. (2) Whether one or multiple base models are required. (3) Which canonical harmful-prompt corpus is the reference set (AdvBench vs HarmBench vs JailbreakBench). (4) The judge of record (lexicon, open classifier, or external LLM). (5) Compute budget / GCG step cap and required parity with prior SSP-defence papers. (6) Token-level adversary surface — editable system prompt or not. (7) Whether benign-helpfulness is a co-primary outcome. (8) Whether multi-turn prompt-level attacks are in-scope.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-23be4dc8-f694-4aa4-9fb1-8330bc73b56e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
